  "decision": "FAIL, by the registered rejection: no threshold step of 1.0 mV appears at depth 0.6 (spike 2 is +0.37 mV above spike 1; spike 10 is +1.5 mV), and the 200 pA count does not fall (4 at both depths, level -64.6 against the reference -65.0 and the recorded -67.1).",
  "observed": "The gate does what it was built to do to the rise: spike 1 unchanged at both inputs (636 to 644 against 639 V/s at 310 pA; 606 to 608 against 598 at 200 pA; first spike within 0.6 ms), and later spikes lose rise progressively, to 0.72 of spike 1 by spike 10 at depth 0.6 (461 against 644 V/s) and 0.83 at depth 0.3. The threshold barely follows: +0.03 and +0.37 mV at spike 2, +0.5 and +1.5 mV at spike 10, against the recorded +1.4 mV at spike 2 and +3.6 by spike 5. At 200 pA the count stays 4, the intervals lengthen by at most 40 ms, and the level after the spikes does not move.",
  "inference": "In this fit a loss of sodium availability moves the rise about 19 percent per millivolt of threshold; the recorded cell moves them together at 4 to 10 percent per millivolt (E: 0.86 of the rise with +1.4 mV at spike 2; I: 0.91 with +4 mV at 0.27 nA). So the fit's threshold is not set by the availability of the sodium it carries, and the recorded step cannot be produced by removing that availability at any depth: the depth that would give +1.4 mV at spike 2 would take the rise far below the recording and does not exist inside the registered range. The shared-function element is not sodium availability alone. What the failure fixes: the recorded threshold step, together with its modest rise loss, needs a threshold that answers to what a spike leaves behind; this fit's threshold answers only to its axonal initiation.",
  "not_run": "Stage 2 (I cell) is not run: the spec applies the surviving depth and none survived. Running the I fit at depth 0.6 would tell whether the threshold insensitivity is this fit's initiation geometry or general; it is proposed, not spent.",
